  - Time and focus: your runs have been dropped by the API after about 50 minutes. Work efficiently, avoid exploratory dead ends, and keep your final report ready; if you are past roughly 35 minutes of work, stop adding scope, make sure what you have compiles and its tests pass, and report.
  - If you cannot reproduce the problem as described, STOP and report what you found instead of inventing a fix.
  - Be precise in your report. Never claim a command passed unless you ran it and saw it pass; list what you could not run.

  TASK (Python: every declaration of a requirement is its own edge, like Cargo).
  Background: the open change in your base tree (crates/callisto-model/src/dependency.rs, DepLocator; crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs; crates/callisto-graph/tests/cargo_declaration_edges_test.rs; .changeset/noble-foxes-climb.md) made every Cargo dependency declaration its own edge, addressed by a DepLocator, and rewritten independently. Read that change first and follow its design. Python still has the original defect, confirmed by an independent reviewer: crates/callisto-manifests/src/python.rs iter_dependencies (around lines 459-557) emits one edge per declaration but they all carry DepLocator::default(), so they share one RewriteKey; the writer update_dependency_spec (around lines 560-700) rewrites only declarations carrying the FIRST declaration's constraint (see the extras block, comment "1b. Extras") and, for [project.dependencies], replaces the first match and breaks.
  Scenarios that must work after the fix:
    A. extras:  [project.optional-dependencies] a = ["b>=1.0"]  c = ["b==1.0.0"]  and b is released at 1.1.0: the loose one stays valid or gets its floor handled as for any plain requirement, and the stale pin "b==1.0.0" is rewritten; neither declaration is rewritten using the other's constraint.
    B. markers: dependencies = ["b==1.0.0; python_version<'3.9'", "b==1.0.0; python_version>='3.9'"]: both lines are rewritten, markers preserved.
    C. the same requirement in [project.dependencies] and in an extra, and in Poetry tables ([tool.poetry.dependencies] and groups) and [dependency-groups] if the code already reads them (do not add new table support).
  Design: extend the locator so Python can say which declaration: the container (project dependencies, an extra by name, a Poetry table) and the position within it. Keep Cargo and npm behavior unchanged. Position is only valid for one plan-then-apply run: validate at write time that the requirement found at that position still has the same normalized name, and fail with the existing DependencyNotFound error instead of rewriting a different declaration. Prefer adding fields to DepLocator with defaults over a new type; if you make the struct #[non_exhaustive] to stop future breaks, provide constructors and migrate every call site (the Cargo code constructs DepLocator with struct-literal syntax; update it). Record every public signature change.
  Steps: (1) reproduce each scenario black-box with the real binary (see crates/callisto-cli/tests/python_dependency_tables_tests.rs and the existing extras tests added for Python extras; model a fixture on them) and at the unit level; show the stale or wrongly-rewritten result. (2) implement. (3) tests: A, B, C, requirement with extras syntax b[x]>=1, normalized-name spellings (Foo_Bar vs foo-bar), a stale locator, format and comment preservation of untouched lines, and Cargo/npm regression tests still passing. (4) scoped verification: callisto-model, callisto-manifests, callisto-graph, callisto-cli, one at a time. Also remove the changeset sentence that says Python requirements declared twice are still followed by their first declaration only: do that by reporting the exact sentence in .changeset/noble-foxes-climb.md that should change (the coordinator edits the changeset); do not edit .changeset files yourself. Suggest changeset text.

